"""Bare USB pads: three SMD pads for a USB cable's data pair and ground, soldered once, so a chip's
USB 2.0 full-speed device port (on the ESP32-C5, its USB Serial/JTAG controller) can be reached
without a receptacle.

Pads in a column at 1.27 mm: 1 GND, 2 D-, 3 D+ (D- before D+ as on the USB connector, USB 2.0
Specification Table 6-1: 1 VBUS, 2 D-, 3 D+, 4 GND; ground at the D- end). The board supplies VBUS's pad, if it takes the cable's 5 V at all: these
pads carry no supply. The silkscreen labels each pad ("G", "D-", "D+") beside it, so the cable goes
on the right way round.

Footprint: three 1.0 mm square SMD pads at 1.27 mm (F.Cu and F.Mask, no paste: nothing is placed by
the fab), a 28-30 AWG wire's tinned end (0.3-0.4 mm) fits one with room for the fillet; the
courtyard is 0.25 mm round the pads. Not a purchased part: copper on the board, excluded from the
BOM and the placement file; no 3D model (nothing is mounted, the wires leave flat).

Integrator-owned (left to the board): series resistors and capacitors on the pair at the chip, ESD
protection, routing as a 90 Ohm differential pair (Espressif ESP32-C5 Hardware Design Guidelines,
Schematic Checklist and PCB Layout Design, USB).
"""

GND = io(Ground)
DN = io(Net, help="pad 2: USB D-")
DP = io(Net, help="pad 3: USB D+")

Component(
    name="J",
    prefix="J",
    symbol=Symbol(library="UsbPads.kicad_sym"),
    footprint=File("UsbPads_1x03_P1.27mm_SMD.kicad_mod"),
    pins={"Pin_1": GND, "Pin_2": DN, "Pin_3": DP},
    skip_bom=True,
    skip_pos=True,
)
